    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize audio and video content for study notes. Communication: Facilitate a discussion on team roles in group work.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can summarize key points from an educational video for study notes, using simple visuals and keywords (e.g., drawing a book to represent learning in the video). Can facilitate a discussion about team roles, using gestures and keywords (e.g., pointing to each team member when assigning roles like, "You research, I write."). Can summarize audio content for study notes, using gestures to illustrate main ideas (e.g., pointing to the head for "thinking" when discussing study habits).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e6ea;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 45%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in "Trimester Check-in." The teacher displays images related to different school subjects (e.g., a math book, a microscope for science, a globe for history). Students briefly share one subject they are currently enjoying or finding challenging, using phrases like "I am learning a lot in history" or "I am interested in improving my math skills." This activates prior knowledge of academic subjects and personal learning experiences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explains the concept of "mediation" by showing a short (30-second) educational video clip about "Good Study Habits." While watching, the teacher explicitly models how to take quick notes using keywords and simple visuals (e.g., drawing a head for "thinking," a book for "reading"). The teacher then summarizes the main idea in one sentence for the class. The teacher also models how to facilitate a short discussion by posing a question like, "What is one good study habit we just heard?" and using gestures (e.g., pointing to students, nodding). The teacher emphasizes stress patterns in modals ("I *would* like to share") and continuous forms ("She *is* writing notes").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is my job when I take notes during the video? (To write *everything* or only *main ideas*?)" (Expected answer: Main ideas).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I ask questions and use gestures in a discussion, what am I doing? (Talking *only* or *helping everyone participate*?)" (Expected answer: Helping everyone participate / Facilitating).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Education and Learning"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short (2-3 minute) educational video or audio clip about "Strategies for Group Work and Collaboration" or "Developing New Academic Skills" (e.g., a simple animated explanation, or a TED-Ed style audio clip). The video/audio should naturally include vocabulary like *skills, knowledge, achieve, develop*, and demonstrate scenarios for *discussing goals* and *offering encouragement*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will summarize key points from the educational video/audio for study notes and identify elements for facilitating a discussion on team roles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students watch/listen to the clip for the first time without taking notes. Then, they answer gist questions orally or with a quick show of hands:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main topic of this video/audio? Is it about sports, or about learning and working together?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you name one *skill* mentioned that helps with group work?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the video/audio say teamwork helps us *achieve* more or less?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Summarizing audio/video content for study notes, facilitating discussion on team roles using present continuous, gerunds after prepositions, and modals for ability/desire. Vocabulary: *education, skills, knowledge, career goals, subject, course, learn, study, develop, grow, achieve, motivated, ambitious, curious, actively, well*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3.1: Guided Note-Taking.</b> Students watch/listen to the educational clip again. This time, the teacher provides a partially filled graphic organizer or template with spaces for keywords and small drawings. Students fill in the blanks/draw simple visuals as they listen/watch, focusing on main ideas. Example prompts: "Main idea about group work: ___ (drawing of people talking)." "Key *skill* for collaboration: ___ (drawing of helping hands)." The teacher pauses the video/audio at key points to allow note-taking, explicitly calling out vocabulary like *develop skills* or *achieve goals*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3.2: Pair Summary and Role Assignment.</b> In pairs, students take turns summarizing different sections of their notes from the educational clip to their partner, practicing the target vocabulary and grammar. For example, "The video says we *are developing* *critical skills* when we work together." "I am *interested in learning* how to communicate *well*." Then, each pair practices facilitating a mini-discussion about team roles based on the video/audio. One student facilitates by suggesting roles using modals ("You *would like* to research, I *can* write the notes."), using gestures (pointing to partner), and asking questions ("How are you doing with finding information?"). The other student responds, potentially discussing their *career goals* related to team *knowledge*. The teacher circulates, providing feedback on pronunciation, especially stress in modals and continuous forms, and minimal pairs like /ɪ/ in *skill* vs /iː/ in *skull*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3.3: Visual Cue Relay.</b> Teacher calls out a key concept from the video (e.g., "Active Listening," "Setting Clear Goals"). In small groups (3-4 students), the first student draws a quick visual cue for the concept. The next student summarizes the concept using the visual and target language (e.g., "This drawing means *actively listening* is important for *developing* good team *skills*."), passing it to the next, until everyone has contributed. This reinforces summarization with visuals and encourages quick, mediated communication.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Education and Learning"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students present their "Learning Vision Board" to a small group of classmates, *mediating* their personal and learning goals. This is the culmination of the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Vision Board Explanation and Goal Facilitation." Students work in groups of 3-4. Each student takes 2-3 minutes to present their "Learning Vision Board," explaining the images, keywords, and goals they have set. They must use present continuous (e.g., "I *am learning* to code online this trimester"), gerunds after prepositions (e.g., "I am *interested in developing* my *critical thinking skills*"), and modals for desires (e.g., "I *would like to study* a new *subject* like *history* more actively").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After each presentation, the presenter facilitates a brief Q&A and discussion (1-2 minutes), asking classmates for suggestions or feedback on their goals, using phrases like "What do you think about my *career goals*?" or "How can I *achieve* this *knowledge* *well*?". Classmates offer encouragement ("You *should keep practicing* to get better!") and suggestions, respecting others' opinions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in mediating their vision board content, collaborative interaction in the discussion, and completion of the goal-sharing task. Students should be able to clearly communicate their personal and learning goals, summarize their aspirations, and engage with their peers regarding these goals.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present and explain their "Learning Vision Board" and facilitate a brief discussion with peers. The teacher will observe and evaluate based on a simple rubric or observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: square; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clearly summarizes/explains the key points of their vision board (Mediation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses present continuous, gerunds, and modals accurately to describe goals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Incorporates at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., *education, skills, knowledge, achieve, motivated*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Facilitates discussion by asking questions or inviting peer input, using gestures as appropriate.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation is intelligible; attempts stress patterns in modals/continuous forms.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages respectfully with classmates' opinions and suggestions (Sociolinguistic).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick self-reflection form:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I explained well on my vision board was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Next time I facilitate a discussion, I will try to..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and look ahead</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new *skills* or *knowledge* are you *interested in developing* for the next trimester, based on today's activities?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can summarizing information and facilitating discussions help you *achieve* your *career goals* in *education*?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of mediating information (summarizing audio/video, facilitating discussion) did students grasp *well*? Were the visual aids and gestures effective in their own mediation? Did the vision board project effectively consolidate the linguistic competences (grammar, vocabulary)? What adjustments are needed for future mediation lessons, particularly regarding scaffolding complex summaries or promoting more proactive facilitation? Did students demonstrate sound discrimination in minimal pairs /ɪ/ and /iː/, and /æ/ and /e/?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "Next Trimester, I Plan to Learn...". The next theme will build on the idea of setting goals and personal development within a different scenario, possibly linking *education* to community engagement or global issues, where students can continue to *develop* their *knowledge* and *skills*.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>